%S A400937 1,9,25,51,85,129,181,243,313,393,481,579,685,801,925,1059,1201,1353,
%T A400937 1513,1683,1861,2049,2245,2451,2665,2889,3121,3363,3613,3873,4141,
%U A400937 4419,4705,5001,5305,5619,5941,6273,6613,6963,7321,7689,8065,8451,8845,9249,9661,10083
%N A400937 Squares visited by knight moves on a diagonally numbered Q1 board and always taking the available unvisited square with the shortest distance to the bisecting line of Q1. In the case of a tie, first squares with the larger row index are preferred, second the square with larger column index is preferred. Starting square is labeled 1.
%C A400937 Board is numbered as follows:
%C A400937   1 2 4 7 11 16 ...
%C A400937   3 5 8 12 17 ...
%C A400937   6 9 13 18 ...
%C A400937   10 14 19 ...
%C A400937   15 20 ...
%C A400937   21 ...
%C A400937   ...
%C A400937 The knight starts at position (1,1). The knight moves in the open field spanned by the positive x- and y-axis, i.e., the first quadrant Q1.
%C A400937 In case the knight has a choice between squares, which have equal distance to the bisecting line, first the square with its row index being larger than the row indices of the alternative squares is preferred. If this first tiebreaking rule still leaves an optional square, the square with its column index being larger than the column index of the alternative square is chosen to break the tie.
%C A400937 Changing the tiebreaking rule in a way that column indices are consider prior to row indices results in the known sequence A204467 (1, 8, 25, 50, 85, 128, 181, 242, 313, 392, ... ) according to computational results.
%H A400937 Benjamin Simon Strang, <a href="/A400937/b400937.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..1300</a>
%H A400937 Benjamin Simon Strang, <a href="/A400937/a400937.svg">Illustration of the path</a>
%H A400937 Benjamin Simon Strang, <a href="/A400937/a400937.py.txt">Python program</a>
%F A400937 a(n) = floor((9*n^2-12*n+6)/2). - _Alper Ferudun_, Oct 17 2026
%F A400937 a(n) = (1/4)*(18*n^2-24*n+11+(-1)^n).
%e A400937 Board after 5 steps: (0's are unvisited squares).
%e A400937   1   0   0   0   0   0   0 ...
%e A400937   0   0   0   0   0   0   0 ...
%e A400937   0   2   0   0   0   0   0 ...
%e A400937   0   0   0   3   0   0   0 ...
%e A400937   0   0   0   0   0   0   0 ...
%e A400937   0   0   0   0   4   0   0 ...
%e A400937   0   0   0   0   0   0   5 ...
%e A400937   ...
%e A400937 a(1) = 1 because 1 is at position (1,1) which is numbered 1.
%e A400937 a(2) = 9 because 2 is at position (3,2) which is numbered 9. In this case, the tiebreaking rule chooses (3,2) over the other available candidate (2,3), because both positions have equal distance to the bisecting line, but (3,2) exhibits the larger row index.
%e A400937 a(3) = 25 because 3 is at position (4,4) which is numbered 25.
